    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the specificity required for dermatological coding in our new billing cycle. We're seeing a lot of cases involving atypical presentations of cutaneous lesions—specifically those with irregular borders, heterogeneous pigmentation, and unusual textural changes that don't clearly fall under standard melanocytic nevus categories. Can you walk me through the hierarchy for selecting the most specific ICD-10 code when the morphology is ambiguous or presents with atypical features?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When documentation of morphological features doesn't straightforwardly align with standard categorizations, the selection of the ICD-10 code should capture the most specific and descriptions possible. In cases with atypical presentations, look for the closest category that depicts the unique characteristics noted. Use subcategories to further refine the code if needed for additional specificity, and always refer to the most recent and comprehensive ICD-10 coding guidelines or seek consultation from a medical coder or dermatology expert. Remember, the priority should be accurate representation of the patient's condition for proper diagnosis and treatment.",
