    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. To get more granular, I'm looking at cases where the lesions have a certain \"gnarled\" or \"pitted\" texture that makes them look almost non-human or distorted compared to a typical lesion. Some of the clinicians are using very descriptive, almost archaic language to describe these irregular growths. If we have a lesion that shows significant structural distortion or an \"alien\" appearance in its surface architecture, is there a specific hierarchy for coding \"unspecified\" morphology versus using a more descriptive code for the anatomical site? We want to avoid denials for lack of specificity.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When encountering pathological changes to the morphology of anatomical sites, the ICD-10 code should capture the specific alteration. Codes for \"unspecified\" morphology may not be as precise and could potentially lead to application denials. It's advisable to use a descriptive code for the anatomical site, even when the altered morphology is mentioned, while also precisely describing the nature of the change. If the altered anatomy significantly impacts diagnosis or treatment, consider using additional coding logic like external cause codes. Always seek consensus from clinical teams, and when in doubt, coding consults or professional resources should be your go-to. Consistence in coding policies and frequent updates to coding knowledge are also crucial in dealing with such atypical cases.",
      "source": "target",
